@@ -0,0 +1,73 @@
+# PlacementRules spec cleanup
+
+This rewrites `PlacementRules.spec.luau` so it checks what `PlacementRules.luau` documents and
+nothing about how it's built. `PlacementRules.luau` itself is unchanged.
+
+## The two failing tests
+
+- **"rejects a position that isn't a Vector3"** called `expect(ok):toBe(false)` with a colon.
+  Jest matchers are plain functions, so the colon passed the expectation object as the expected
+  value, and the test compared `false` to a table. The fix is to use a dot. luau-lsp didn't catch
+  this because `expect` isn't strongly typed.
+- **"doesn't mark the cell occupied"** counted the keys in the table and asserted
+  `expect(count).toBeFalsy()`. In Luau `0` is truthy, so this failed even though `validate` left
+  the table alone. It now checks `next(occupied)` is nil, plus a second case on the reject path.
+
+## Removed
+
+- **"calls snap exactly once"** spied on `PlacementRules.snap`. That's an implementation detail:
+  inlining or caching the snap would break it without changing behavior. The snapped center is
+  already checked through `validate`'s return value.
+- **The `_isFinite` tests (3)** tested a private helper. They're replaced by `validate` cases with
+  NaN, `+inf`, and `-inf` components. The old suite only covered NaN through `validate`.
+  `_isFinite` is still exported because this change doesn't touch the module, so that export can
+  now be removed.
+- **"snaps positions to cell centers"** worked out its expected values with the same
+  `floor(v / size) + 0.5` formula as the code, so it could never fail. The table test with fixed
+  inputs and outputs replaces it.
+- **"BlockTypes has the right block types"** checked colors, materials, and the order of the
+  block types. That's game content, not a placement rule, and any content tweak broke it.
+  `validate` still runs against every entry in `BlockTypes.List`.
+- **"rejects placing twice in the same cell"** repeated the occupied test and only passed because
+  an earlier test had written into a shared, file-level `occupied` table. That table is gone, so
+  every test now builds its own state.
+
+## Changed
+
+- **No exact reason strings.** The docs only promise "a short reason for the server log", so
+  rewording a message used to break tests. Rejections now assert `false` plus a non-empty string.
+  Each rejection case changes one input from a request that's known to pass, so it can only fail
+  on the rule being tested.
+- **Boundaries come from the module's constants.** Height and reach cases use
+  `PlacementRules.MaxHeight`, `PlacementRules.MaxReach`, and `BlockTypes.BlockSize` instead of
+  hard-coded 200, 24, and 4. The reach cases place the root relative to the cell's center, so the
+  "exactly MaxReach is in reach" case holds whatever the grid size is.
+- **Snap cases** are written in cell indices (`cellCenter(x, y, z)`), and the origin row covers
+  the documented "the cell resting on the Baseplate is centered at Y = BlockSize / 2".
+
+## Coverage of the documented rules
+
+| Documented rule | Covered by |
+|---|---|
+| `snap` returns the center of the cell containing the position | `snap` table: origin, cell center, far edge, cell boundary, negatives |
+| Known block type | every `BlockTypes.List` entry accepted; unknown name, nil, and a number rejected |
+| Position is a Vector3 | nil and a table rejected |
+| Finite components | NaN, `+inf`, and `-inf` rejected |
+| Above the Baseplate, no higher than MaxHeight | lowest and highest valid cells accepted; the cells just outside them rejected |
+| Has a character | nil root rejected (**new**, this wasn't tested before) |
+| Within MaxReach, inclusive | exactly MaxReach accepted; just past it rejected; diagonal past it rejected (straight-line distance) |
+| Cell not occupied | a point inside an occupied cell rejected; a free cell beside an occupied one accepted |
+| Returns true and the cell's center | the accept case checks the returned center |
+| Never changes `occupied` | checked on both the accept and the reject paths |
+
+The suite still has 31 tests. The difference is that each one now checks a documented rule.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- The docs say a cell must be "above the Baseplate (Y > 0)", but the code rejects `center.Y < 0`.
+  Cell centers are always odd multiples of `BlockSize / 2`, so a center can never be exactly 0
+  and the two readings behave the same. The specs test the cells either side of the boundary
+  rather than Y = 0 itself.
+- Specs can't run in this environment. The suite passes `luau-lsp analyze`, `selene`, and
+  `stylua --check`, but nobody has run it in Studio yet. It uses only `describe`, `it`,
+  `it.each`, and the matchers `toBe`, `toEqual`, `toBeNil`, and `toBeGreaterThan`.
